    englishMeaning: "Arjuna said: O Supreme Person (Purushottama), what is that Brahman? What is Adhyatma (the inner Self)? What is Karma (action)? What is called Adhibhuta (the material realm)? And what is said to be Adhidaiva (the celestial realm)? With this verse begins Chapter 8, 'Akshara Brahma Yoga' (The Yoga of the Imperishable Absolute). Following Sri Krishna's closing statements in Chapter 7, Arjuna acts as the archetypal spiritual inquirer, asking precise questions that unlock the deepest metaphysical truths: 1. Address to Purushottama: Arjuna addresses Sri Krishna as Purushottama (the Supreme Transcendental Being), acknowledging that only the Supreme Lord Himself can authoritatively resolve these cosmic mysteries. 2. The Five Foundational Questions: What is Brahman? What is Adhyatma? What is Karma? What is Adhibhuta? What is Adhidaiva? These inquiries form the cornerstone of Vedantic metaphysics.",
